<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Agadez</h1><p class="st-meta">1449 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Agadez</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sultanate of the Air mountains in the Sahara, whose sultan arbitrated among Tuareg confederations and guarded caravan routes between Hausaland and North Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> confederations of the Air massif agreed in the early 15th century to accept a sultan who stood outside their rivalries; tradition says the first came from the north or even from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. The seat moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a> Shaman to the oasis town of Agadez around 1449, and the town grew as a caravan halt.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan was chosen from a single family but installed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> leaders, whose consent limited his power. He collected tolls on caravans, judged disputes and led the salt caravans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilma">Bilma</a>. Agadez had a mixed Tuareg and Hausa-speaking population, Muslim scholars, and a tall mud-brick minaret. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a> made the sultanate a tributary around 1500.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foureau-lamy-mission">Foureau-Lamy mission</a> passed through Agadez in 1899, and French forces occupied the town in the early 1900s. In 1916 and 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegama">Tegama</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaocen-ag-mohammed">Kaocen</a>, which besieged the French garrison; he was captured and killed. The sultanate survives in a ceremonial form, and Agadez's old town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-agadez.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Agadez" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: En rouge · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LocationT%C3%A9n%C3%A9r%C3%A9.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1449 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Agadez</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tegama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamasheq and Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Niger</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Air Sultanate, Sultanate of Air, Agadez</dd></dl></aside></div>
